WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.730
- One topic where I
regularly get questions

00:00:02.730 --> 00:00:04.340
from you is networking.

00:00:04.340 --> 00:00:07.210
Many people believe that
being good at networking

00:00:07.210 --> 00:00:11.320
is essential to land that high
profile job like consulting,

00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:13.570
investment banking, or a big corporate.

00:00:13.570 --> 00:00:17.320
Though in my experience,
there is a wide misconception

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:18.970
when it comes to networking

00:00:18.970 --> 00:00:22.100
and this is exactly what I'm
going to talk about today.

00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:25.890
I will tell you why most people
have networking all wrong

00:00:25.890 --> 00:00:29.140
and I will tell you how instead
you should use networking

00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:31.193
to really make it benefit your career.

00:00:31.193 --> 00:00:33.070
Welcome to another coffee break

00:00:33.070 --> 00:00:35.160
here on my channel Firm Learning.

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:36.410
My name is Heinrich.

00:00:36.410 --> 00:00:39.650
I am a former consultant at
McKinsey and at Accenture

00:00:39.650 --> 00:00:42.940
and on my channel I want to
help you to become successful

00:00:42.940 --> 00:00:44.890
in the first years of your career.

00:00:44.890 --> 00:00:48.250
On my channel, I want to pass
on the experience that I have

00:00:48.250 --> 00:00:50.340
from working and consulting at McKinsey

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:52.820
and also at other companies
throughout my career.

00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:55.260
Recently I asked for video suggestions

00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:57.430
on my Instagram page, firmlearning,

00:00:57.430 --> 00:01:00.490
and many of you asked me
to talk about networking.

00:01:00.490 --> 00:01:03.630
There really is this
perception that networking

00:01:03.630 --> 00:01:07.180
is a crucial component
of doing your job hunt,

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:09.950
of getting the dream
job that you wanna get.

00:01:09.950 --> 00:01:13.250
I know that people invest a
lot of time on career events

00:01:13.250 --> 00:01:15.010
and similar activities like that

00:01:15.010 --> 00:01:17.830
just to get in contact
with the HR professionals

00:01:17.830 --> 00:01:20.770
who are actual other
employees of the companies

00:01:20.770 --> 00:01:22.120
that you are interested to join.

00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:25.150
And I was on the receiving
side of this as well.

00:01:25.150 --> 00:01:26.460
So, on several instances,

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:28.980
I attended career events as faculty.

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:31.420
So, as a consultant of the firm,

00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:34.370
but then just organized and
conducted the career event

00:01:34.370 --> 00:01:35.440
and as you can imagine,

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:38.100
during the social phases of
that event, for instance,

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:39.750
during the evening so then later,

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:42.360
also the night outs that
are often being organized,

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:45.220
all the attendees and
participants would contact you

00:01:45.220 --> 00:01:46.053
as a consultant.

00:01:46.053 --> 00:01:48.730
Of course, a lot try to get
you into a conversation,

00:01:48.730 --> 00:01:51.210
ask you about your job,
ask about the careers,

00:01:51.210 --> 00:01:52.950
asking you whether you could help them

00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:54.880
to get the interview to get in.

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:56.600
And this, of course, is perfectly normal.

00:01:56.600 --> 00:01:57.990
This is perfectly okay.

00:01:57.990 --> 00:01:59.370
I behaved like that as well

00:01:59.370 --> 00:02:01.730
when I was a candidate
trying to land a job.

00:02:01.730 --> 00:02:03.990
That being said, I do think that there is

00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:07.770
a major misperception
of many of these people

00:02:07.770 --> 00:02:09.610
who attend these events are engaged

00:02:09.610 --> 00:02:11.600
in similar networking activities.

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:13.510
And this misperception is that if you,

00:02:13.510 --> 00:02:16.300
as a candidate, manage
to make a good impression

00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:18.657
on the HR professional, on the consultant

00:02:18.657 --> 00:02:21.680
attending these events,
then they would help you

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:24.040
to get the interview, to get invited,

00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:26.260
just because they like you so much.

00:02:26.260 --> 00:02:29.230
So, pretty much believing that
networking is a door opener

00:02:29.230 --> 00:02:31.580
that helps you to get in, to get the job,

00:02:31.580 --> 00:02:32.610
to get into the interview.

00:02:32.610 --> 00:02:35.460
But, I can tell you that this is wrong.

00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:37.620
This is not how it works.

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:39.330
And while, of course, there might always

00:02:39.330 --> 00:02:40.900
be individual exceptions,

00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:43.710
in general, you will not get invited

00:02:43.710 --> 00:02:46.690
to the interview only because
on any of these events,

00:02:46.690 --> 00:02:49.257
you made a good impression
on the HR professional

00:02:49.257 --> 00:02:51.770
or on a consultant just in
a personal conversation.

00:02:51.770 --> 00:02:53.490
The leading consulting firms,

00:02:53.490 --> 00:02:55.900
and I trust many other firms as well,

00:02:55.900 --> 00:02:59.180
have very strong processes in place

00:02:59.180 --> 00:03:01.690
to prevent that people just get invited

00:03:01.690 --> 00:03:05.610
to the interview due to
personnel relations in the firm.

00:03:05.610 --> 00:03:08.510
Only because your best buddy
is a consultant working

00:03:08.510 --> 00:03:09.740
with one of these firms,

00:03:09.740 --> 00:03:12.010
this alone will not get you in

00:03:12.010 --> 00:03:13.330
and if you don't believe me,

00:03:13.330 --> 00:03:14.970
just think about it, right?

00:03:14.970 --> 00:03:17.710
How does the whole process works?

00:03:17.710 --> 00:03:20.240
Because you send in your
application documents.

00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:21.790
And then, of course, we go through

00:03:21.790 --> 00:03:23.900
a pretty solid review process.

00:03:23.900 --> 00:03:26.000
As you can imagine, in most regions,

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:29.800
there are lots of HR professionals
who screen these CVs.

00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:32.590
You can by no means assume
that the HR professional

00:03:32.590 --> 00:03:34.650
that you talked will actually be the one

00:03:34.650 --> 00:03:35.820
who screens your CV.

00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:37.760
Even if that professional does allow you

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:40.470
to directly send your documents
to that person instead of

00:03:40.470 --> 00:03:42.258
the regular online process,

00:03:42.258 --> 00:03:43.980
still this does not regularly mean

00:03:43.980 --> 00:03:45.930
that this person will also be the one

00:03:45.930 --> 00:03:49.100
who in the end screens your
CV and decides on your case.

00:03:49.100 --> 00:03:50.970
And besides that, also put yourself

00:03:50.970 --> 00:03:52.670
in the shoes of these people.

00:03:52.670 --> 00:03:55.110
These ladies and gents attend these kinds

00:03:55.110 --> 00:03:57.290
of events every other day

00:03:57.290 --> 00:04:00.450
and every single time
they will be approached

00:04:00.450 --> 00:04:02.490
by a whole bunch of candidates,

00:04:02.490 --> 00:04:06.080
a whole bunch of interests
that people who just will try

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:07.710
to make a conversation with them.

00:04:07.710 --> 00:04:09.290
Will try to talk to them.

00:04:09.290 --> 00:04:11.720
Will try to make an impression on them.

00:04:11.720 --> 00:04:14.180
Even if you do your
very best and do indeed

00:04:14.180 --> 00:04:16.510
have a good conversation with that person,

00:04:16.510 --> 00:04:19.060
how high do you think
your chances really are

00:04:19.060 --> 00:04:22.330
that you will make a lasting
impression on that individual?

00:04:22.330 --> 00:04:23.940
Why this might not be impossible,

00:04:23.940 --> 00:04:26.870
this for sure shouldn't be
something that you trust you will

00:04:26.870 --> 00:04:28.510
be accomplishing in these meetings.

00:04:28.510 --> 00:04:30.470
And the same is true for the consultants.

00:04:30.470 --> 00:04:32.769
So, not necessarily the HR professionals,

00:04:32.769 --> 00:04:35.280
but the consultants who just
often also attend these events.

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:37.610
These guys will be even farther away

00:04:37.610 --> 00:04:40.490
from the actual decision
process, because first of all,

00:04:40.490 --> 00:04:42.620
they're usually not involved at all

00:04:42.620 --> 00:04:44.750
in screening CVs of candidates.

00:04:44.750 --> 00:04:48.040
So, they will usually
have no leverage to help

00:04:48.040 --> 00:04:50.710
you get through the CV screening process.

00:04:50.710 --> 00:04:51.810
But, what if you don't manage

00:04:51.810 --> 00:04:53.740
to get through the CV screening

00:04:53.740 --> 00:04:55.100
and get into the interviews?

00:04:55.100 --> 00:04:58.100
Because there indeed it is
true that these are conducted

00:04:58.100 --> 00:04:59.350
by actual consultants.

00:04:59.350 --> 00:05:01.620
But, the chance that
you will be interviewed

00:05:01.620 --> 00:05:04.320
by the same consultant
that you randomly meet

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:07.430
on one of these events
will be super small,

00:05:07.430 --> 00:05:09.650
at least in a large office like Germany,

00:05:09.650 --> 00:05:12.040
they're literally hundreds of consultants

00:05:12.040 --> 00:05:13.190
doing these interviews,

00:05:13.190 --> 00:05:15.880
you by no means will have the
certainty that this would then

00:05:15.880 --> 00:05:18.370
also be the guy who does
the interview with you.

00:05:18.370 --> 00:05:20.430
So, I hope that by now
we're on the same page

00:05:20.430 --> 00:05:22.570
and this perception that many people have,

00:05:22.570 --> 00:05:24.510
that if you network you will get invited

00:05:24.510 --> 00:05:27.350
to interview and that also
help you then get the job

00:05:27.350 --> 00:05:30.480
that this in most instances is wrong

00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:33.570
and for sure is wrong
for MBB consulting firms

00:05:33.570 --> 00:05:35.140
like McKinsey, BCG, or Bain.

00:05:35.140 --> 00:05:37.460
Still, my view on that is not

00:05:37.460 --> 00:05:39.420
that there is no value in networking.

00:05:39.420 --> 00:05:42.530
I believe there is a very specific place

00:05:42.530 --> 00:05:44.850
of networking in your application journey

00:05:44.850 --> 00:05:45.830
and for this reason,

00:05:45.830 --> 00:05:47.820
I actually think that it is quite crucial

00:05:47.820 --> 00:05:49.950
and quite important for most candidates

00:05:49.950 --> 00:05:52.930
to engage in some kind
of networking activities

00:05:52.930 --> 00:05:54.550
of the like that I've just described.

00:05:54.550 --> 00:05:57.960
So, to better understand what
networking actually is for

00:05:57.960 --> 00:05:59.570
in the whole recruiting process,

00:05:59.570 --> 00:06:02.360
is to understand what
actually most people struggle

00:06:02.360 --> 00:06:06.440
with most when they try to
land this high-profile job

00:06:06.440 --> 00:06:07.890
that we're all looking out for.

00:06:07.890 --> 00:06:09.860
So, when I talk to people who prepare

00:06:09.860 --> 00:06:11.590
for their consulting interviews,

00:06:11.590 --> 00:06:13.320
many of them tell me, well, you know,

00:06:13.320 --> 00:06:15.370
I practiced all of these cases.

00:06:15.370 --> 00:06:18.470
I learned all of these
frameworks by heart.

00:06:18.470 --> 00:06:22.450
I know exactly what to say in
these interviews and so on.

00:06:22.450 --> 00:06:24.240
And, of course, this is important

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:26.730
and this is crucial to
get the interview right.

00:06:26.730 --> 00:06:30.130
But still, from my experience
when working with candidates

00:06:30.130 --> 00:06:33.840
the number one reason that
holds people back really

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:35.330
is the mindset.

00:06:35.330 --> 00:06:36.890
Many people, if you really have

00:06:36.890 --> 00:06:38.750
a heart-to-heart conversation with them,

00:06:38.750 --> 00:06:41.020
we'll tell you that they actually believe

00:06:41.020 --> 00:06:42.510
that they are not good enough

00:06:42.510 --> 00:06:44.830
for a job at an MBB consulting firm.

00:06:44.830 --> 00:06:48.390
The criteria that are often
also communicated by these firms

00:06:48.390 --> 00:06:51.090
that you need to meet to
get in sometimes seems

00:06:51.090 --> 00:06:52.880
so difficult to reach.

00:06:52.880 --> 00:06:56.150
They seem so elusive
that people just believe

00:06:56.150 --> 00:07:00.090
that no way they are good
enough, no way they can do it,

00:07:00.090 --> 00:07:03.010
no way they have what it
takes to become a consultant

00:07:03.010 --> 00:07:05.550
in one of these large consulting firms

00:07:05.550 --> 00:07:07.537
or to get into that
other high profile career

00:07:07.537 --> 00:07:09.400
that they actually would like to.

00:07:09.400 --> 00:07:11.640
And this, of course, is a huge problem.

00:07:11.640 --> 00:07:13.730
If you are not convinced
that you are good enough,

00:07:13.730 --> 00:07:16.530
how are you supposed to put together a CV

00:07:16.530 --> 00:07:18.220
that can convince anybody else

00:07:18.220 --> 00:07:20.270
that you are the right person for the job?

00:07:20.270 --> 00:07:23.510
If you lack the confidence
that you would be a great fit

00:07:23.510 --> 00:07:25.650
to work at McKinsey, BCG, or Bain,

00:07:25.650 --> 00:07:28.550
how will you convince
your interviewer of this?

00:07:28.550 --> 00:07:31.550
To get yourself ready
for these interviews,

00:07:31.550 --> 00:07:34.640
to give yourself a chance
to get into these jobs,

00:07:34.640 --> 00:07:37.040
you need to work on your mindset first.

00:07:37.040 --> 00:07:39.130
You need to build that confidence.

00:07:39.130 --> 00:07:41.280
You need to build that
conviction, that yes,

00:07:41.280 --> 00:07:44.050
you have a good and
solid chance to get in.

00:07:44.050 --> 00:07:47.130
That yes, you would be able to do the job,

00:07:47.130 --> 00:07:50.080
that you would be in a
position to add value

00:07:50.080 --> 00:07:52.470
at a consulting team or whatever company

00:07:52.470 --> 00:07:53.730
you would fancy to get in.

00:07:53.730 --> 00:07:56.310
And this is now where from my experience,

00:07:56.310 --> 00:08:00.400
networking can play a crucial
role in the whole process.

00:08:00.400 --> 00:08:01.390
On these events,

00:08:01.390 --> 00:08:03.130
you have the opportunity to talk

00:08:03.130 --> 00:08:06.970
to these real consultants, to
real employees of these firms

00:08:06.970 --> 00:08:09.070
and if you have a good
conversation with them,

00:08:09.070 --> 00:08:11.070
you have the opportunity to realize

00:08:11.070 --> 00:08:13.210
that they are actually very normal,

00:08:13.210 --> 00:08:15.500
very down to earth people.

00:08:15.500 --> 00:08:16.980
They are not that different,

00:08:16.980 --> 00:08:18.450
probably from any friends

00:08:18.450 --> 00:08:20.350
that you also surround yourself with.

00:08:20.350 --> 00:08:22.580
They are not that different from you.

00:08:22.580 --> 00:08:25.530
They also studied at a
university probably not

00:08:25.530 --> 00:08:28.570
that different to yours, they're
have probably good grades.

00:08:28.570 --> 00:08:32.140
Not everybody is in the
top 3% of their class

00:08:32.140 --> 00:08:33.790
who joined one of these firms.

00:08:33.790 --> 00:08:36.930
They are not people from another planet.

00:08:36.930 --> 00:08:38.940
There are people like you.

00:08:38.940 --> 00:08:41.160
You can be one of these guys as well.

00:08:41.160 --> 00:08:43.130
And the more you talk to these people,

00:08:43.130 --> 00:08:45.640
the easier it will be for
you to understand this

00:08:45.640 --> 00:08:47.440
and to really get this into your head

00:08:47.440 --> 00:08:50.830
and it will hopefully enable
you to come to the conclusion

00:08:50.830 --> 00:08:52.550
that yes, it could be you as well.

00:08:52.550 --> 00:08:54.280
You could do this job as well.

00:08:54.280 --> 00:08:56.220
There is nothing that these people have

00:08:56.220 --> 00:08:57.700
that you don't have as well,

00:08:57.700 --> 00:08:59.880
because indeed here, the truth is,

00:08:59.880 --> 00:09:01.990
everybody's just cooking with water.

00:09:01.990 --> 00:09:04.460
Indeed there is no magic ingredient.

00:09:04.460 --> 00:09:07.080
And sometimes indeed these
firms can be quite good

00:09:07.080 --> 00:09:10.030
at making you feel like it
is this way, but in the end,

00:09:10.030 --> 00:09:12.090
trust me from experience,
you don't need to have

00:09:12.090 --> 00:09:15.370
an IQ of 150 plus to be able to do the job

00:09:15.370 --> 00:09:16.850
and to do a good job at it.

00:09:16.850 --> 00:09:20.580
So, use networking events
to really understand

00:09:20.580 --> 00:09:24.130
what the reality of working in
one of these firms look like,

00:09:24.130 --> 00:09:26.430
how the people are that you
might surround yourself with

00:09:26.430 --> 00:09:27.810
there in the future.

00:09:27.810 --> 00:09:30.670
And use this as a reflection for yourself.

00:09:30.670 --> 00:09:32.230
Can you be one of these guys as well?

00:09:32.230 --> 00:09:34.710
Do you want to be one
of these guys as well?

00:09:34.710 --> 00:09:36.980
Is this a job that would
really interest you

00:09:36.980 --> 00:09:39.730
and that you do believe
that you can add value.

00:09:39.730 --> 00:09:41.000
And with this conviction,

00:09:41.000 --> 00:09:44.320
I trust that interviews
will be much easier for you

00:09:44.320 --> 00:09:47.120
and also trust that this
will significantly help you

00:09:47.120 --> 00:09:48.780
to come up with a strong CV.

00:09:48.780 --> 00:09:49.960
And frankly in the end,

00:09:49.960 --> 00:09:52.990
this is also a big part of
what I try to accomplish

00:09:52.990 --> 00:09:54.330
with my channel as well.

00:09:54.330 --> 00:09:57.410
I hope that with my videos
I make it more transparent

00:09:57.410 --> 00:10:00.080
what it actually looks like
to be a consultant also

00:10:00.080 --> 00:10:01.890
in one of these high profile firms.

00:10:01.890 --> 00:10:04.300
And I do hope that you
realize that actually

00:10:04.300 --> 00:10:07.700
I am not that different
than many of you guys

00:10:07.700 --> 00:10:11.270
and I trust that many of
you would be very well able

00:10:11.270 --> 00:10:14.030
to do this job as well if you, of course,

00:10:14.030 --> 00:10:15.320
want to do it in the end.

00:10:15.320 --> 00:10:17.020
So, I hope this does make sense to you.

00:10:17.020 --> 00:10:19.010
And as always, if you have any question,

00:10:19.010 --> 00:10:21.500
leave me a comment below
in the comment section

00:10:21.500 --> 00:10:23.700
and please be aware that
if you write the comment,

00:10:23.700 --> 00:10:25.770
not only will I do my best to provide you

00:10:25.770 --> 00:10:27.030
with an insightful answer,

00:10:27.030 --> 00:10:30.350
but you're really supporting
me with the YouTube algorithm

00:10:30.350 --> 00:10:32.100
because a video with lots of comments

00:10:32.100 --> 00:10:34.340
always looks great to the system.

00:10:34.340 --> 00:10:35.317
And in addition, if you took any value out

00:10:35.317 --> 00:10:37.430
of this video at all,

00:10:37.430 --> 00:10:40.180
please hit the like button
for the YouTube algorithm.

00:10:40.180 --> 00:10:42.380
And please also subscribe to my channel

00:10:42.380 --> 00:10:44.300
to stay up to date on all my content.

